+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers correct the same plot visit independently, on tablets that can be offline for 48 hours. Two edits that started from the same state of a visit can therefore reach the record store days apart, and the server must say what the visit's state is afterwards. Arrival order says nothing about which worker's observations are right.
+
+Whatever the record store does here fixes three things the rest of TidePlot is built around: what a tablet must remember and send with each edit, what a coordinator is asked to review, and which visits may enter the Friday CSV for the science partner. A wrong choice either puts values in the export that no person looked at together, or leaves two coordinators with more review work than they can clear before Friday.
+
+Every option keeps all original submissions; that is a property of the record store, not something this decision trades.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates an option in contention. The weighted criteria:
+
+- **Related edits stay together — major.** A visit's fields carry scientific relationships the server cannot see: a revised species identification belongs with its cover estimate; an observer change may explain a changed estimate. An exported visit should not hold a combination of values that no person ever saw side by side. Derives from the non-goal *no automated inference of scientific intent* and the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+- **Review workload and export readiness — major.** Two coordinators clear all review work, and a visit that is not resolved by Friday misses that week's CSV. Derives from the goal *make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export* and the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate*.
+- **Rules to maintain — moderate.** Logic that decides how edits combine must be kept correct as the visit's fields change, by a team with no dedicated systems administrator. Derives from the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Explaining an exported value — moderate.** How much a coordinator must reconstruct to answer "where did this number come from?". Derives from the inspectability tenet.
+- **Reversibility — moderate.** Whether the data kept under one option is enough to adopt the other later without losing history.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size — minor.** At about 6,000 visits a season on 12 tablets, neither option strains storage or connectivity; it differs only in how much state tablet-sync manages.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each edit is a complete copy of the visit and names the revision it started from. An edit that starts from the visit's current revision replaces it. An edit that starts from anything else competes with the current revision: the visit goes into review with the competing revisions side by side and has no usable state until a coordinator selects one or supplies a replacement. The server compares revision IDs only; it never looks inside the fields to decide an outcome.
+
+- **Related edits stay together — strong.** A revision is exactly what one worker submitted. Fields from different submissions are only ever combined by a coordinator, and that combination is stored under the coordinator's name.
+- **Review workload and export readiness — weak.** Every concurrent pair goes to review, including an observer-name fix alongside an added species that could never conflict. Each such visit is held out of the CSV until resolved.
+- **Rules to maintain — strong.** The only rule is base-revision equality. Adding a field to the visit changes nothing here.
+- **Explaining an exported value — strong.** An exported visit is one revision with one author, or one coordinator resolution naming the revisions it settled.
+- **Reversibility — strong.** Each revision holds the complete fields and its base revision ID, which is everything a three-way merge needs. Merge could be introduced later, and even replayed over past history.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size — strong.** A tablet keeps one revision ID per draft and sends the visit it already holds.
+
+Summary: maximal preservation of what each worker meant, paid for in coordinator time.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each edit supplies the state it started from and the changes it makes. The server compares both edits against their common starting state. Changes to different fields combine automatically into a new current state; changes to the same field go to review, and only for that field. Combining needs rules per kind of field: scalars such as observation date and notes, the observer list (order, additions, removals), and species entries (matching an entry across edits, an entry removed by one edit and changed by another).
+
+- **Related edits stay together — weak.** A species re-identification from one worker and a cover estimate from another are different fields and combine without review. Declaring groups of related fields narrows this but is itself a rule set that encodes scientific intent and can only cover relationships someone anticipated.
+- **Review workload and export readiness — strong.** Only overlapping changes reach a coordinator, and the review is of one field rather than a whole visit. More visits are ready on Friday.
+- **Rules to maintain — weak.** List and species-entry merging are the hard cases, and every new or changed field needs a rule. A wrong rule produces plausible-looking wrong data rather than an error.
+- **Explaining an exported value — partial.** Fully traceable only with per-field provenance: which submission each exported value came from, and which rule combined them. That is additional stored state and an additional view to build.
+- **Reversibility — partial.** Moving to whole-visit revisions later is possible if complete base snapshots were kept, but visits already merged stay merged; their combined states were never reviewed as a whole.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size — partial.** A tablet keeps a base snapshot per draft and builds structured changes against it.
+
+Summary: least coordinator time, paid for in rules that can be wrong silently.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The two major criteria point in opposite directions, and the choice is to take the larger review queue. A missed Friday is visible and recoverable: the visit is listed as in review and goes out the following week. A merged visit that joins a species identification to someone else's cover estimate is neither; it exports looking like any other row. The decision turns on the inspectability tenet and on the non-goal of inferring scientific intent, and is taken knowingly against some of the weight of *workflows two coordinators can operate*, which the review queue strains and the absence of merge rules relieves.
+
+Reversibility settles what the major criteria leave close. Revisions keep everything a merge would need, so this choice can be revisited with real data; the reverse move could not un-merge what had already been combined.
+
+**When to reconsider field-aware merge.** How often workers edit the same visit concurrently, and how often those edits touch unrelated fields, is not known. The option becomes the better one if:
+
+- coordinators regularly cannot clear the review listing before Friday, so that visits miss exports because of queue length rather than real disagreement; and
+- the revision history shows that most competing pairs change disjoint fields and that coordinators resolve them by combining both unchanged.
+
+Both are measurable from data this design already keeps: the listing of visits in review, and resolution records compared with the revisions they settled. A narrower step is available before full merge: auto-combining only field pairs that resolution history shows coordinators always combine, which keeps the rule set small and evidence-led.
+
+**What this forecloses.** No visit reaches an export in a state that combines two workers' fields unless a coordinator made that combination. Review volume cannot be reduced by server logic; it can only be reduced by fewer concurrent edits or faster review.
+
+**What tablet-sync must accommodate.**
+
+- Keep the base revision ID with every local draft, across restarts and multi-day disconnection, and send it with the complete visit.
+- Treat "accepted into review" as a normal outcome of a submission, distinct from rejection, and tell the worker their edit is kept and awaiting a coordinator.
+- Handle a visit that has no usable state: show its competing revisions as pending rather than presenting any one as the visit.
+- Expect a draft based on a revision that lost a resolution to reopen review when submitted.
+- No base snapshots and no change-building are needed.
+
+**What review-export must accommodate.**
+
+- A review queue sized for every concurrent pair, not only real disagreements, with whole-visit comparison of the competing revisions against each other as its main screen.
+- Fast paths for the cheap cases the queue will be full of: identical revisions, and revisions that differ in unrelated fields, where the coordinator's replacement is simply both edits.
+- Resolutions that name the revisions the coordinator was shown, and a re-review when another revision arrived in the meantime.
+- Export eligibility of `settled` visits only, with the visits held out of a given Friday's CSV visible to coordinators before it is produced.
+- A stated behaviour for a visit that was exported while settled and later returns to review.
